<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Huanca</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1460 CE (approx.) · capital Hatun Xauxa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of highland peoples in Peru's Mantaro valley who fought among themselves until conquered by the Incas, and later allied with the Spanish.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a> collapsed, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mantaro-valley">Mantaro</a> valley in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was divided among the Huanca, or Wanka, and related groups such as the Xauxa. From about 1000 they formed communities of chiefdoms, and in the 14th century fortified hilltop towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunanmarca">Tunanmarca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatunmarca">Hatunmarca</a> grew to house thousands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Huanca were divided into rival groups led by local war leaders called sinchis, and their hilltop towns reflect frequent warfare. They farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and other tubers, grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> in lower valleys and herded llamas. The valley was one of the most productive in the central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/andecavi">Andes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatun-xauxa">Hatun Xauxa</a> later became a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> conquered the Huanca around 1460, resettling them in valley-floor villages. In 1533 Huanca lords allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and supplied his troops; they later petitioned the Spanish crown for rewards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jauja">Jauja</a> was briefly the Spanish capital in 1534.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/huanca.jpg" alt="Huanca" width="257" height="340"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pe_ehuan.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1460 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hatun Xauxa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Huanca religion, cult of Wari Willka</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Quechua (Wanka)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wanka, Xauxa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1230&amp;focus=huanca" aria-label="Huanca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2680 2653 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Huanca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3000 2909h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3000 2909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=1230&amp;focus=huanca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

